<p class="lead">For most HVAC companies, the <a href="/integrations/google-business-profile">Google Business Profile</a> brings in more calls than the website. Profiles that show fresh, real work in the right towns tend to look more trustworthy to customers. The problem is time: nobody in the office has an hour a day to write posts. Here is how to turn the photos your technicians already take into Google Business Profile posts automatically.</p>
<h2 id="why-job-photos-are-the-best-content-you-have">Why job photos are the best content you have</h2>
<p>A photo of a new unit installed in a real home, in a real town, tells customers what you do and where you do it. It shows real equipment, real work and a real location. Stock photos and generic tips don't do any of that.</p>
<h2 id="the-flow">The flow</h2>
<ol>
  <li>The tech texts a photo to a company number after a job. No new app to learn.</li>
  <li>The system reads the photo to identify the equipment and type of work, and uses the job's location to name the town.</li>
  <li>It drafts a short post in plain language, mentioning the service and the area.</li>
  <li>An owner or manager approves it with one tap, or edits it.</li>
  <li>It publishes to Google Business Profile, and to Facebook and Instagram if you want.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="keep-a-person-in-the-loop">Keep a person in the loop</h2>
<p>An approval step catches photos that show a customer's face, house number or anything private. It also stops awkward wording before it goes public. It takes seconds per post.</p>
<h2 id="rules-that-keep-posts-useful">Rules that keep posts useful</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Mention the town or area, never the customer's street address.</li>
  <li>Keep posts short and specific: what was done and why it matters.</li>
  <li>Don't post the same wording twice; vary it by job type.</li>
  <li>Post steadily, rather than ten in one day and nothing for a month.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="what-changes-in-practice">What changes in practice</h2>
<p>We built this for <a href="/case-studies/red-dragon-hvac">Red Dragon HVAC</a>. Technicians text photos through <a href="/integrations/twilio">Twilio</a>; the system identifies the work, writes the post and sends it for approval before publishing through the Google Business Profile API. Local leads grew, posts got more engagement, and every technician used it. See our <a href="/solutions/home-services">home services page</a> for more.</p>
